From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 14:10:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] media: venus: disable wrapper clock auto-gating on msm8998

The msm8998 wedge is closed, and it was one register the whole time.

WRAPPER_CLOCK_CONFIG (wrapper_base + 0x04) powers on as 0x007f001f on
msm8998: internal clock auto-gating that leaves the VBIF register page
and everything above wrapper+0x1000 unable to answer the config bus. The
first CPU access into any of those pages -- read or write, measured both
-- stalls the MMSS NoC hard enough that only the watchdog recovers, with
nothing logged. That is the silent death every bisect arm has been
mapping: the preset write to base+0x80124 was only the first such access
mainline happens to make.

Proof, on a Pixel 2 XL via /dev/mem with the probe parked right after
power-on (stop_at=10), all clocks CCF-verified running, all three GDSCs
on:

  read  wrapper+0x00           0x30430709   (control, always worked)
  read  wrapper+0x04           0x007f001f   (the reset value)
  write wrapper+0x04 <- 0
  read  wrapper+0x2010         0x00000010   (wedged every prior arm)
  read  base+0x80000           0x200a0000   (wedged every prior arm)
  read  base+0x80124           0x00000003   (wedged every prior arm)
  write base+0x80124 <- 0x3, readback OK    (THE write that killed 11+ boots)

The vendor driver zeroes WRAPPER_CLOCK_CONFIG and WRAPPER_CPU_CLOCK_CONFIG
in __prepare_enable_clks() on every power-on, before its first venus
register access, on all of its SoCs. msm8996 evidently powers on
permissive, which is why mainline never hit this there. Do the same at
the top of venus_run(), before venus_set_registers() touches VBIF.

Scoped to 3XX for now: the 4XX+ wrapper layout differs and those SoCs
demonstrably do not need it.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/hfi_venus.c | 19 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/hfi_venus.c b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/hfi_venus.c
index f5d5abb33c07..659d13ecb306 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/hfi_venus.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/hfi_venus.c
@@ -631,6 +631,25 @@ static int venus_run(struct venus_hfi_device *hdev)
 	if (venus_stop_boot(2))
 		return 0;
 
+	/*
+	 * msm8998's wrapper powers on with internal clock auto-gating
+	 * (WRAPPER_CLOCK_CONFIG = 0x007f001f) that leaves the VBIF page and
+	 * everything above wrapper+0x1000 unable to answer the config bus:
+	 * the first CPU access into them -- read or write, measured both --
+	 * stalls the MMSS NoC below the level printk can report, and only
+	 * the watchdog recovers. That was the whole msm8998 wedge.
+	 *
+	 * The vendor zeroes both gating registers in __prepare_enable_clks()
+	 * on every power-on before its first register access; msm8996 powers
+	 * on permissive, which is why mainline never needed this before.
+	 * Write them before venus_set_registers() touches VBIF. Scoped to
+	 * 3XX: the 4XX+ wrapper layout differs and those SoCs boot without.
+	 */
+	if (IS_V3(hdev->core)) {
+		writel(0, hdev->core->wrapper_base + WRAPPER_CLOCK_CONFIG);
+		writel(0, hdev->core->wrapper_base + WRAPPER_CPU_CLOCK_CONFIG);
+	}
+
 	if (!presets_early)
 		venus_set_registers(hdev);
 
